<p>A few days ago, I was rambling about <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/07/27/style-emacs-config-a-semantic.html">style and semantic HTML</a>. It was a post mostly about <code>&lt;kbd&gt;&lt;/kbd&gt;</code> tags, but toward the end I said this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Reading through their tomb of standards, I realized I need to correct my headings on the blog too (I jump directly to &lt;h3&gt;&lt;/h3&gt; tags instead of going by order, because the CSS for these tags looks better — in other words, I&rsquo;ve been lazy by not fixing this), among potentially many other things. There are over a hundred semantic tags in HTML, and there&rsquo;s a reason for each one.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&ldquo;Their tomb of standards&rdquo; refers to the <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-h1,-h2,-h3,-h4,-h5,-and-h6-elements">HTML Living Standard</a>, and they (among other sources) explain why sub-headings in posts <em>should</em> actually be <code>&lt;h3&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;</code> tags (the way I&rsquo;ve done it so far). Pretty simple when you think about it.</p>
<ol>
<li>The main header of the entire website is <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> (in my case, &ldquo;The Art of Not Asking Why&rdquo;).</li>
<li>Blog post headings are <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> (as in the heading of this post, above).</li>
<li>Blog post sub-headings are <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>, as I was doing it.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is not just a style and organizational thing; this is also how web browsers are programmed to read web pages, and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/Heading_Elements">they expect</a> one <code>&lt;h1&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;</code> pair.</p>
<p>You&rsquo;d notice I didn&rsquo;t use the word &ldquo;title&rdquo; above, because in HTML the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> is reserved for metadata in the head part of the page (defined by <code>&lt;head&gt;&lt;/head&gt;</code>). It&rsquo;s there, and it serves a purpose — it&rsquo;s basically your website&rsquo;s name or the title of your entire web document (we need to remember, these things originated and were based back on what was printed on paper).</p>
<p>OK. I was wrong and order is restored. If I want to do sub-sub-headings, there&rsquo;s <code>&lt;h4&gt;</code> for that. But if I get to that level of breaking down my posts, I think it&rsquo;s a good sign that I&rsquo;ve been writing too much in one go and should consider a smaller post.</p>
